{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Now there was found in it a poor man</b> (ומצא בה איש מסכן): It is an adjective, and מסכנות is the noun, and the mem is added. This form is known in Aramaic. And the meaning of <b>and no man remembered</b> (ואדם לא זכר) is that there was no remembrance of that poor wise man in the mouths of the people of the city beforehand, before he saved them. There are some grammarians who say that זכר is not in the mouth, and the matter is that every זכר and ויזכור is remembrance of the heart, whereas the word אזכירה, which is in the heavy [causative] binyan, is in the mouth — and they needed to explain it thus. And <b>the chief cupbearer did not remember him</b> (ולא זכר שר המשקים) [means] that he did not bring him to mind, and <b>and he forgot him</b> (וישכחהו) is a redundant expression. And it seems more correct in my eyes that the word זכירה is sometimes in the heart and sometimes in the mouth, and <b>and the burden of HaShem you shall mention no more</b> (ומשא יקוק לא תזכירו עוד) indicates this, whose meaning is: you shall no longer bring the burden of HaShem to remembrance in the mouth — for what does <b>you shall mention no more</b> have to do with <b>it shall not come to your minds</b> (כי מה ענין לא יעלה על לבבכם זכר משא)?"], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The verse citation in the closing proof text (Jeremiah 23:36) is implicit; the Hebrew phrases ומשא יקוק לא תזכירו עוד and לא יעלה על לבבכם זכר משא are rendered exactly as Ibn Ezra quotes them."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:47.907232499Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}